0235: malformed FIFO data no longer stops the game (issue #17: "command_processor: unknown opcode
0x1D at pos 11946", fatal on every start afterwards).

- The command processor checks each command before carrying it out: an unknown opcode, a draw with
  an impossible primitive or more vertex data than the block holds, a truncated BP/CP/XF/indexed
  load, an XF load past the end, a known Aurora subcommand that does not fit or carries an impossible
  draw, an indexed XF load from an unmapped array. Each is reported (ERROR log with the absolute
  stream position, its offset in the block, the bytes skipped and 64 bytes before / 32 after; the
  first 10, then at most one a second, with a count of those not shown) and through
  AuroraSetFifoErrorCallback (the application names the guest code that wrote it), then processing
  resumes at the next position where at least 8 plausible commands parse in a row (exact opcodes,
  known CP/XF addresses, sane counts, Aurora subcommand lengths) or commands parse to the exact end
  of the block; otherwise the rest of the block (one publish) is dropped. Draws of 0 vertices and
  strips/fans of fewer than 3 are skipped (their index buffer size underflowed).
- Only a stream that stays broken is fatal, with a clear message: AURORA_FIFO_ERROR_LIMIT errors
  (default 300) within 10 s.
- Recordings stop at the first write that does not fit (they used to drop only that write and go
  on, leaving a list cut inside a command); GXEndDisplayList returns 0 for a list that overflowed,
  as the SDK does.
- AuroraCaptureJoin: a thread writes into the capture (or display list) another thread began last.
  Emulated guest threads share the console's one CPU FIFO; a guest thread other than the capturing
  one used to write into the FIFO directly, at the same time as the host thread's replays.
- AuroraFifoStreamPos: absolute stream position of the next byte the calling thread writes.

